<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shoumeng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 585–561 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as King Shoumeng of Wu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shoumeng was king of Wu from 585 to 561 BCE, the first ruler of the state recorded with firm dates. Under him Wu, on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, opened relations with the central states and, with Jin's help through the Chu exile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-wuchen">Qu Wuchen</a>, adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> warfare and began attacking Chu. His sons included Jizha, famous for refusing the throne.</p></div>
